  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 507 PM MDT Tue Oct 20 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview: \n\nUpper-level ridging will allow for mild conditions to continue \nthrough Thursday. Temperatures will be warmer than average and winds \nwill be lighter with gusts generally remaining under 25 mph.\n\nA weather system out of the Pacific that has been lingering over the \nwest coast will begin to track eastward on Thursday, bringing cooler \ntemperatures and moisture to the area. A cold front associated with \nthis system will move through the region on Friday, resulting in the \nhigh temperatures on Friday to occur during the morning hours. \nAdditionally, post frontal winds will develop Friday afternoon and \ncontinue through Friday night. The highest gusts are expected along \nthe Rocky Mountain Front and Central MountainS. For southwest \nMontana, there is also a chance for thunderstorms due to passing of \nthe cold front during the day on Friday. Due to snow levels not \nfalling until the precipitation associated with this system has \ndecreased, the bulk of snowfall is likely to be limited to the \nmountains. Any snowfall that does mix-in for the lowland areas will \nbe hindered in accumulating due to recent rainfall and warm \ntemperatures.\n\nLooking beyond Saturday, although confidence is lower due to the how \nfar into the future this is, upper-level troughing looks like it \nwill remain in place over the region. Overall, temperatures will \ncontinue to be cooler with overnight freezes each night. Winds \nwill be mild across the region with gusts generally under 25 mph. \nAn upper-level trough has the potential to clip north Montana on \nSunday and cause some light showers. With snow levels still at \nlower levels and daytime high temps in the upper 30s/low 40s, \nlight showers may be snow or a snow/rain mix in the lowlands. -ESH\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nHigh confidence for areas of mountain snowfall Thursday night \nthrough Saturday morning. 60-80% chance of 2\" or more during that \ntime frame. Main areas impacted will be the northern Rockies, Island \nMountains, and SW Montana mountains.\n\nMedium confidence for whether there will be snowfall in lowland \nareas on Friday night and Saturday due to an offset in snow levels \nfalling and precipitation timing. Currently there is a <30% chance \nfor a trace of snowfall in lowland areas. -ESH\n\nA possible alternate scenario that would allow for more lowland \nsnow: The low/trough phasing is slower, both delaying the \nprogression of the troughing but also allowing deformation moisture \nto linger across the area while cold air drives snow levels down \ntowards 3kft. This will introduce a window for a more persistent but \nlight snowfall across lower elevations. That being said, warm ground \ntemperatures and hours of rain proceeding the potential change would \nrestrict any of this to elevated surface if any at all. -JR",
